      "content": "If you're uncertain or it's difficult to disconnect, from a distance, you might want to grab your camera before you touch it, just in case. Slowly pry the P-trap apart, looking for hidden clogs. If everything glistening like a greasy swamp and it looks really off, it's okay to contact a professional - safety first. Any visible twisty bits? Replaced dripping p-trap parts? Found a sewer leak under the sink connecting to the faucet, sink, deck p-traps under other cooking stations? Check for that too! Do NOT reassemble the P-trap without removing the foul substance. A dirty trap smells just as putrid, seeing no accessibility to that gunk complicates things. Remove it from the building system thoroughly – soak if stuck. Next, I would recommend contacting a professional if things do not look right. They have the specialized tools for digging out troublesome blockages and ensuring everything is back in working order without causing more strain on your plumbing system. It's best to leave the task at that stage if you're unsure about confronting what you find. This not only prevents potential damage from your efforts but saves the professional time and resources they might face with unskilled interventions. Safety precautions must come mainly when dealing with",
